  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the runner on an umbrella?",
      "a": "The runner is the collar that slides up and down the shaft. Pushing it up opens the umbrella, and sliding it down folds it.",
      "asks": [
        "How does an umbrella open?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are the ribs?",
      "a": "Ribs are the long thin arms hinged at the top of the shaft that hold the canopy out. They swing up and out as the umbrella opens.",
      "asks": [
        "What holds the canopy out?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the stretchers do?",
      "a": "Stretchers are short struts linking the runner to each rib. As the runner rises, they push the ribs outward, like the links of a linkage.",
      "asks": [
        "How do the ribs spread?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What holds an umbrella open?",
      "a": "A spring catch on the shaft pops out under the runner once it is pushed up, stopping it sliding back down. Pressing the catch releases it.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the button on an umbrella?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do umbrellas turn inside out in the wind?",
      "a": "A strong gust from below pushes the canopy up harder than the stretchers can hold, so the ribs swing past their hinges the wrong way. Vented and flexible ribs help prevent it.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my umbrella flip?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Umbrellas and sunshades have been used for over 3,000 years, in ancient Egypt, Assyria and China.",
    "An umbrella's ribs, stretchers and runner form a linkage that turns a straight slide into a spreading motion."
